00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.027
John Mark Wallach: Welcome,
and thank you for joining me.

00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:29.446
Today we're gonna talk about
accessibility, not only as a compliance

00:03:29.446 --> 00:03:32.340
issue, but as a business strategy.

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:38.746
My central argument is simple:
accessibility is growth,

00:03:39.378 --> 00:03:44.744
accessibility is experience, and
accessibility is a revenue engine

00:03:45.061 --> 00:03:47.943
when organizations treat it that way.

00:03:48.579 --> 00:03:49.501
Next, please.

00:03:51.538 --> 00:03:55.269
So my perspective is both
professional and personal.

00:03:56.275 --> 00:04:02.458
I am the CEO of Engagement Mobile
Strategies, where we build exclusively

00:04:02.461 --> 00:04:08.439
on WordPress, and I'm also living
with ALS and fully dependent on

00:04:08.476 --> 00:04:11.151
accessible technology every day.

00:04:12.228 --> 00:04:16.718
So when I talk about accessibility,
I'm not speaking about an

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.966
abstract best practice.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:26.666
I'm talking about access, independence,
engagement, and participation.

00:04:28.262 --> 00:04:34.032
One quick request before we move
on is keep your cellphone handy.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.501
If you find value in what I'm going to
share today and want to continue the

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:45.067
conversation, I'll give you an easy
way to connect with me near the end.

00:04:45.843 --> 00:04:46.363
Next.

00:04:48.232 --> 00:04:51.141
So the accessibility myth, what is that?

00:04:51.727 --> 00:04:56.881
Well, the accessibility myth belongs
only in the compliance column.

00:04:57.599 --> 00:05:01.894
It's a myth that we're only
doing this just to be compliant.

00:05:01.951 --> 00:05:04.167
And yes, compliance matters.

00:05:05.228 --> 00:05:07.571
Risk reduction matters.

00:05:08.188 --> 00:05:10.394
Regulations matter.

00:05:10.695 --> 00:05:14.011
But if we stop there, we
miss the bigger opportunity.

00:05:14.758 --> 00:05:20.047
Now, for associations and nonprofits,
accessibility can mean higher member

00:05:20.047 --> 00:05:27.227
engagement, greater participation,
stronger retention, and more revenue from

00:05:27.227 --> 00:05:32.099
registrations, memberships, and donations.

00:05:32.518 --> 00:05:37.174
Sponsorships grow as well, as
well as other pro- uh, programs.

00:05:37.942 --> 00:05:45.710
Now, for corporations, it means increased
customer loyalty and more revenue.

00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:50.715
Accessibility is not merely
a cost of doing business.

00:05:51.399 --> 00:05:53.486
It can be a driver of growth

00:05:56.666 --> 00:06:00.716
So let's talk about the digital
curb cut effect, and we'll start

00:06:00.716 --> 00:06:02.575
with the regular curb cut effect.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.722
You're probably all aware of it.

00:06:04.732 --> 00:06:10.652
The curb cut eff- uh, is that little
part in the corner of every city block

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:16.713
that's been cut out to allow access, and
they were designed for wheelchair users.

00:06:17.965 --> 00:06:22.665
But what's interesting is they
help parents with strollers,

00:06:23.196 --> 00:06:24.765
travelers with luggage.

00:06:25.185 --> 00:06:29.971
Delivery workers use them over and over
again, as well as countless others.

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:35.096
The digital accessibility
works the same way.

00:06:36.139 --> 00:06:42.082
Captions, voice interfaces,
responsive design, and better search

00:06:42.084 --> 00:06:47.481
functionality may start as your
accessibility improvements, but

00:06:47.481 --> 00:06:49.698
they end up benefiting everyone.

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:54.544
Designed for some, but benefit everyone.

00:06:56.473 --> 00:07:02.467
So the audience is changing, and what
ma- that matters, uh, financially.

00:07:03.175 --> 00:07:08.991
Baby boomers are aging, but they
still represent an enormous amount

00:07:09.047 --> 00:07:14.012
of purchasing power, membership
value, and institutional knowledge.

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:20.014
If we make digital experiences harder
for them, we shorten our lifetime

00:07:20.017 --> 00:07:22.742
value of those relationships.

00:07:23.672 --> 00:07:26.702
Think about something as
simple as color contrast.

00:07:27.108 --> 00:07:31.050
If someone cannot clearly see
the menu, the navigation, or

00:07:31.051 --> 00:07:36.951
the call to action, they cannot
engage with what they cannot find.

00:07:37.664 --> 00:07:45.861
Better contrast, larger text, clearer
navigation, and simpler interfaces help

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:52.881
people stay active longer as members,
as customers, donors, or participants.

00:07:53.826 --> 00:07:55.402
And then there

00:07:57.609 --> 00:08:06.397
is Gen Z. They expect digital experiences
to be immediate, intuitive, and flexible.

00:08:06.706 --> 00:08:11.242
Accessibility features increasingly
overlap with the way they

00:08:11.243 --> 00:08:13.174
already consume information.

00:08:14.002 --> 00:08:15.515
Here's a quick example.

00:08:16.502 --> 00:08:22.758
How many of you are already watching
Netflix or other streaming services

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:28.844
with the closed captions on even though
you can hear the audio perfectly?

00:08:29.615 --> 00:08:33.592
As a matter of fact, drop a
thumbs up in the chat if you can.

00:08:34.139 --> 00:08:35.881
Well, that's the curb cut effect.

00:08:36.573 --> 00:08:43.192
It's helping everyone when it was
built for a few to be compliant.

00:08:43.557 --> 00:08:49.471
It now allows all of your
generations to enjoy the balance

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:52.823
of a more accessible website

00:08:55.837 --> 00:08:59.032
So this is what digital
friction feels like.

00:08:59.235 --> 00:09:05.592
Tiny fonts, poor contrast, broken
navigation, missing captions.

00:09:06.072 --> 00:09:08.607
For some users, these are annoyances.

00:09:08.941 --> 00:09:11.192
For others, they are blockers.

00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:16.686
Either the way they create
frustration, confusion, or abandonment.

00:09:17.545 --> 00:09:23.641
Every one of those problems
can turn into an interesting,

00:09:24.448 --> 00:09:26.648
uh, an interested visitor away.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:28.073
Let me repeat that.

00:09:29.073 --> 00:09:35.649
Any one of those problems can turn someone
that's interested in your goods and

00:09:35.649 --> 00:09:39.291
services, an interested visitor, away.

00:09:41.853 --> 00:09:44.415
And every click has a cost.

00:09:44.945 --> 00:09:48.173
Every moment of confusion is costly.

00:09:48.676 --> 00:09:56.500
Every inaccessible form, menu,
video, or PDF, it asks the users

00:09:56.501 --> 00:09:58.426
to work harder than they should.

00:09:59.078 --> 00:10:03.789
I face that every day when I'm
on sites that create so much

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.187
friction that I give up sometimes.

00:10:07.005 --> 00:10:12.454
And for an association, that means
members, a member who does not renew, or

00:10:12.456 --> 00:10:18.187
a prospect who never registers, or a donor
that abandons the process, just like I do.

00:10:18.959 --> 00:10:23.942
Um, for a corporation, it can
mean a customer who simply goes

00:10:24.224 --> 00:10:28.771
somewhere else, and I'm that
customer that'll go somewhere else.

00:10:29.283 --> 00:10:34.101
So when the experience is
difficult, those bounce rates rise,

00:10:34.353 --> 00:10:37.714
engagement falls, revenue declines.

00:10:38.156 --> 00:10:40.740
Accessibility reduces that cost.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:47.705
So when we make accessibility
improvements, we reduce friction.

00:10:48.498 --> 00:10:52.234
Larger, higher contrast
helps people read faster.

00:10:52.787 --> 00:10:57.700
Keyboard navigation helps those
that cannot use a mouse, but it

00:10:57.702 --> 00:11:03.168
also helps those power users that
would rather use the keyboard to

00:11:03.168 --> 00:11:04.968
advance throughout their screen.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:11.202
Captions, well, they help the deaf users,
but they also help anyone watching in a

00:11:11.202 --> 00:11:14.516
noisy room or environment without sound.

00:11:15.569 --> 00:11:22.331
Better accessibility creates
faster decisions, greater

00:11:22.347 --> 00:11:25.497
confidence, and better experience.

00:11:28.723 --> 00:11:33.773
Accessibility improves engagement
'cause people stay where they

00:11:34.022 --> 00:11:36.647
feel welcomed and understood.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:45.130
Remember, your digital information
is designed to engage these

00:11:45.135 --> 00:11:47.626
people, not obstruct them.

00:11:48.217 --> 00:11:54.441
So for associations and nonprofits, better
accessibility can mean members spending

00:11:54.445 --> 00:12:00.416
more time with content, registering
for more programs, renewing more

00:12:00.423 --> 00:12:08.344
consistently, donating more easily, and
engaging across more of the organization.

00:12:08.986 --> 00:12:14.418
If you're a corporation, it can
mean more page views, stronger

00:12:14.458 --> 00:12:17.722
conversion, repeat purchases.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:22.751
Man, it's so great when you get a
customer once, but it's better when

00:12:22.753 --> 00:12:25.148
they turn into reoccurring revenue.

00:12:26.055 --> 00:12:29.717
Um, and greater customer
loyalty happens from this.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:36.436
If the site's easier to read,
navigate, search, and act on,

00:12:37.034 --> 00:12:39.048
people spend more time there.

00:12:39.734 --> 00:12:45.878
They explore more, they sign up
more, and they come back more often.

00:12:46.264 --> 00:12:50.753
That is where accessibility
begins to connect directly to

00:12:50.753 --> 00:12:52.925
lifetime value and revenue.

00:12:55.545 --> 00:12:57.911
It also improves discoverability.

00:12:58.479 --> 00:13:04.027
How many times have you said, "Oh, I
wish we were higher ranked on our SEO"?

00:13:04.433 --> 00:13:11.372
And now with agents and answer
engines, you wish you were

00:13:11.383 --> 00:13:14.181
being picked up by the LLMs.

00:13:14.583 --> 00:13:19.573
Well, accessibility improves
that discoverability.

00:13:20.114 --> 00:13:26.913
Many of the accessibility practices
overlap with strong search practices,

00:13:27.227 --> 00:13:35.658
structures, metadata, alt text, semantic
HTML, captions, and clean content.

00:13:36.447 --> 00:13:41.431
The way SEO works is in
that organized fashion.

00:13:41.441 --> 00:13:49.691
The way agentic optimization works is
in that organized fashion, and it's the

00:13:49.693 --> 00:13:54.263
same fashion as being WCAG compliant.

00:13:55.179 --> 00:13:59.706
These help people understand
your site, and they help the

00:13:59.707 --> 00:14:01.835
machines understand it, too.

00:14:02.412 --> 00:14:09.284
And that matters for the search engines,
AI assistants, and answer agents.

00:14:11.672 --> 00:14:15.254
So why search engines love accessibility?

00:14:16.056 --> 00:14:20.203
Well, search engines love the
clarity, and so do people.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:27.136
Headings, structure, metadata,
and alt data, they're not just

00:14:27.187 --> 00:14:30.467
technical details, they're signals.

00:14:30.944 --> 00:14:37.395
They tell people and machines what
your content is, why it matters,

00:14:37.647 --> 00:14:39.601
and how it should be understood.

00:14:40.345 --> 00:14:43.811
Better structure creates better access.

00:14:46.362 --> 00:14:49.506
Now, AI makes this even more important.

00:14:49.566 --> 00:14:54.959
The AI assistants and voice
assistants, search engines, and

00:14:54.959 --> 00:14:59.084
knowledge graphs all depend on
content that they can understand.

00:14:59.968 --> 00:15:03.416
Accessible Word sites
are better organized.

00:15:03.994 --> 00:15:04.622
Uh, pardon me.

00:15:04.622 --> 00:15:09.377
Accessible WordPress sites
are better organized, better

00:15:09.404 --> 00:15:12.351
labeled, and easier to interpret.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:18.626
That means you get better
answers, greater visibility, and

00:15:18.803 --> 00:15:21.207
increased engagement over time.

00:15:24.066 --> 00:15:30.702
So WordPress gives us a strong
foundation, but it's not automatic.

00:15:31.515 --> 00:15:37.107
Themes, plugins, content, and
governance, they all matter.

00:15:37.652 --> 00:15:43.545
The flexibility of WordPress
is powerful, but flexibility

00:15:43.561 --> 00:15:46.282
also requires responsibility.

00:15:46.986 --> 00:15:54.133
Accessibility has to be considered in
the tools we choose, the content we

00:15:54.133 --> 00:15:57.926
create, and the process we maintain.

00:15:59.934 --> 00:16:05.592
And at EMS, we choose WordPress
because it aligns with how we

00:16:05.606 --> 00:16:07.919
want organizations to work.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:15.836
We have a, uh, tagline here where
it's our job to empower, engage, and

00:16:15.839 --> 00:16:23.061
transform, and it empowers them using
WordPress, and it helps them engage,

00:16:23.062 --> 00:16:28.730
as I've talked about, um, but it
also gives you the room to transform.

00:16:29.273 --> 00:16:35.887
The open ecosystem extensibility
and accessibility focus make it

00:16:35.904 --> 00:16:40.358
sh- a strong, strong platform
when it's used thoughtfully.

00:16:40.454 --> 00:16:45.493
And WordPress gives organizations
control, but that control has to

00:16:45.498 --> 00:16:47.506
be paired with good decisions.

00:16:48.492 --> 00:16:54.557
Accessibility mistakes we see most
often, these are the ones we see.

00:16:55.869 --> 00:17:01.793
They're missing alt text or
don't have proper alt text.

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.041
The color contrast is poor.

00:17:05.957 --> 00:17:12.817
We have improper headings, keyboard
navigation issues, and uncaptioned media.

00:17:14.005 --> 00:17:17.992
Now, let's take these
a few at a time here.

00:17:18.955 --> 00:17:20.458
Missing alt text.

00:17:21.101 --> 00:17:29.684
Let's say you have a photo of a woman
smartly dressed with a yellow purse that

00:17:30.027 --> 00:17:39.585
also allows her to have her computer
in the purse as well, and all your alt

00:17:39.612 --> 00:17:47.199
text is either is nothing, so nobody
with a screen reader can see it, or it

00:17:47.206 --> 00:17:50.394
just says, "Woman with yellow purse."

00:17:51.796 --> 00:18:00.693
Now, what if that was reset, re-said, uh,
in the alt text where it said, "Smartly

00:18:00.695 --> 00:18:07.972
dressed individual holding a yellow purse
that can also double as a computer bag"?

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:15.845
Well, that gives a phenomenal image of
really what the functionality is of that

00:18:15.853 --> 00:18:18.051
image and what it's trying to convey.

00:18:18.597 --> 00:18:23.746
And if you're in the business of selling
yellow purses that hold computers, not

00:18:23.746 --> 00:18:30.115
only will the few people that are using
their screen readers see in their mind

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:33.494
what it is, so will those alt engines.

00:18:33.542 --> 00:18:35.092
Um, and pardon me.

00:18:35.093 --> 00:18:39.129
So will those search engines 'cause
they're reading your alt text.

00:18:40.039 --> 00:18:45.774
So will those answer engines,
which are those agents that people

00:18:45.775 --> 00:18:48.964
are now using to identify things.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:55.136
So you're g- gonna have phenomenal
ranking even though you made it

00:18:55.716 --> 00:18:59.872
compliant and increased the alt text.

00:19:00.606 --> 00:19:02.036
Poor color contrast.

00:19:02.037 --> 00:19:06.533
You know, I, I explained I have ALS,
but this has nothing to do with ALS.

00:19:07.183 --> 00:19:12.587
If I go to a restaurant and the lighting's
low, I'm 65, I have trouble reading,

00:19:12.908 --> 00:19:20.839
uh, in some of those dark environments
when there's not real contrast or

00:19:21.294 --> 00:19:27.553
the, the font is too fancy, so it
doesn't create contrast on the page.

00:19:27.901 --> 00:19:33.754
Or the background of the page has this
beautiful vine working on it, and then

00:19:34.294 --> 00:19:39.083
how will I be able to, how will I be able
to distinguish that from the letters?

00:19:39.531 --> 00:19:44.680
So that poor color contrast
limits lots of people.

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:49.152
Young, old, not necessarily disabled.

00:19:49.529 --> 00:19:56.029
But by making it compliant, you've
now made it broader for everyone.

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:58.476
And improper headings.

00:19:58.476 --> 00:20:04.599
When your headings are structured
properly, not only can someone go

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:09.773
through your page and understand it,
especially if they're using a reader,

00:20:10.328 --> 00:20:17.685
but also, again, those agents that will
increase your ranking and determine

00:20:17.685 --> 00:20:22.841
that your site is more credible
will have an easier time reading it.

00:20:23.545 --> 00:20:26.360
And then there's keyboard
navigation issues.

00:20:26.793 --> 00:20:31.352
You know, those little four arrows
that are at the bottom of every

00:20:31.369 --> 00:20:38.085
keyboard keypad, you're supposed
to be able to navigate with those.

00:20:38.702 --> 00:20:42.929
And so someone that's not using
a mouse but using those arrows to

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:49.364
navigate, you need to make sure that
your keyboard, uh, navigation is fine.

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:54.722
Now, why is that important beyond
just those that have some disability?

00:20:54.724 --> 00:20:57.414
Well, powers- power users.

00:20:57.955 --> 00:21:03.549
They use those arrows instead of the
mouse 'cause they can actually operate

00:21:03.677 --> 00:21:08.600
faster using those arrows, taking
them to exactly where they want.

00:21:09.435 --> 00:21:13.484
And you're gonna lose those
people because you are not

00:21:13.538 --> 00:21:15.924
accessible in that, in that way.

00:21:16.609 --> 00:21:20.074
And then of course, uncaptioned media.

00:21:20.765 --> 00:21:28.608
The uncaptioned media is anything that
might be audible, but if you're deaf

00:21:28.626 --> 00:21:33.121
you can't read it, you, you … and
there's no captions, you, you can't,

00:21:33.189 --> 00:21:34.985
you can't tell what's being said.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:40.714
More importantly though, what if
you have someone that's riding on

00:21:40.716 --> 00:21:46.806
a train and doing it with the sound
off, but wants to read along the way.

00:21:47.382 --> 00:21:54.175
Uh, what if you have a customer
or a member that just by nature

00:21:54.673 --> 00:21:59.249
chooses to use the captions
instead of listening, especially if

00:21:59.251 --> 00:22:03.199
they're in an environment where the
sound itself could be disruptive?

00:22:04.562 --> 00:22:11.357
Well, not having that captioned
media will in turn, uh, not make you

00:22:11.383 --> 00:22:17.906
compliant, but also not make you, uh,
valuable to that broader audience.

00:22:18.451 --> 00:22:21.659
So none of these are exotic.

00:22:22.339 --> 00:22:24.188
As a matter of fact, they're common.

00:22:24.878 --> 00:22:31.625
And as I know, they're fixable, easily
fixable, that if you have any bandwidth

00:22:31.627 --> 00:22:37.560
on your team, it can be done quite
quickly, and they're often overlooked.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:44.801
But together they shape whether someone
feels invited in or pushed away.

00:22:50.302 --> 00:22:53.234
I think of accessibility
as a maturity curve.

00:22:53.804 --> 00:22:59.619
First comes awareness, then
compliance, then experience.

00:23:00.121 --> 00:23:04.580
After that, followed by
optimization, and finally, growth.

00:23:06.079 --> 00:23:11.396
What we found is too many
organizations, they stop at compliance.

00:23:11.851 --> 00:23:14.518
We did just enough to be compliant.

00:23:15.291 --> 00:23:19.796
I'll give you an example outside
of the digital environment.

00:23:20.284 --> 00:23:25.758
But when I go to hotels, I,
uh, get an accessible room.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:32.653
I always find it interesting that the
shower bench is four feet away from

00:23:32.653 --> 00:23:38.649
the shower controls, so I'm trying to
figure out how someone that needs to

00:23:38.649 --> 00:23:43.876
roll into the shower can turn on the
shower without getting their chair wet.

00:23:44.653 --> 00:23:51.086
So that's just a little thing, but we're
finding the same thing that happens i-

00:23:51.086 --> 00:23:56.952
in accessibility, because that hotel has
checked off the boxes for accessibility.

00:23:57.575 --> 00:24:01.231
But when it comes to digital
accessibility, you can check off

00:24:01.251 --> 00:24:06.588
the boxes, but are you taking it
now beyond just being compliant?

00:24:07.517 --> 00:24:13.842
Too many organizations stop there, and
the real opportunity is moving towards,

00:24:15.998 --> 00:24:20.798
are we creating the best
possible experience for the

00:24:20.801 --> 00:24:23.249
widest possible audience?

00:24:24.404 --> 00:24:26.676
So let me give you a practical framework.

00:24:27.654 --> 00:24:34.973
We like the framework of evaluate,
remediate, monitor, and improve.

00:24:36.028 --> 00:24:42.235
And one way to really control
that is to have a process in

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:44.907
place for each of these items.

00:24:45.904 --> 00:24:47.665
So how do we evaluate?

00:24:47.712 --> 00:24:54.976
Well, we use, uh, uh, n- some
automated engines, but we also

00:24:54.979 --> 00:25:03.022
have a real-life person overview
to see how things are functioning.

00:25:03.619 --> 00:25:09.158
So internally, if you just decided
to use on your Apple the screen

00:25:09.158 --> 00:25:13.782
reader, you could tell if your
page is organized well enough.

00:25:14.253 --> 00:25:22.405
Or you could pay a consultant
to do an audit or a monitoring

00:25:22.407 --> 00:25:26.856
of your site to provide you with
the various code violations.

00:25:27.312 --> 00:25:32.358
Or you could do something in between,
where you, uh, use one of these

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:37.447
LLMs to help you evaluate, uh,
what you have, and then determine

00:25:37.447 --> 00:25:42.241
if you have the bandwidth to go to
the next phase, which is remediate.

00:25:42.882 --> 00:25:53.164
Now, remediate is something that I
find becomes less and less once you

00:25:53.166 --> 00:25:55.574
have this practical framework in place.

00:25:56.053 --> 00:25:58.050
But remediation is really simple.

00:25:59.331 --> 00:26:02.208
One example is the "click here".

00:26:02.958 --> 00:26:09.326
You might have five buttons on your
page that all say "click here".

00:26:09.814 --> 00:26:14.149
Well, how would you like to be
r- hearing, "Click here, click

00:26:14.167 --> 00:26:15.985
here, click here, click here"?

00:26:17.662 --> 00:26:22.047
And not know what each
one goes to whatsoever.

00:26:23.276 --> 00:26:30.482
But if one said, "Click here for more
information about how to become a

00:26:30.483 --> 00:26:38.230
member," or the other one said, "Click
here to learn how to become part of

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:44.347
our loyalty program," or another one
said, "Click here," whatever the subject

00:26:44.347 --> 00:26:52.312
was, well, each one of those will
now help the person using the screen

00:26:52.312 --> 00:27:00.149
reader as well as the various agents
to identify what that's connecting to,

00:27:00.603 --> 00:27:02.842
uh, and giving it more credibility.

00:27:03.337 --> 00:27:08.828
Now, we can evaluate that by going
to our page and just looking how many

00:27:08.975 --> 00:27:13.931
click here buttons do we have that
don't have the ending of the subject.

00:27:13.931 --> 00:27:16.001
In other words, click here for what?

00:27:16.893 --> 00:27:20.212
And then you go in and
just remediate the button.

00:27:20.831 --> 00:27:24.384
Quite frankly, if you're on
WordPress, that's so easy.

00:27:24.922 --> 00:27:30.745
But regardless of what CMS you're on,
it's easy, uh, or very little time

00:27:30.749 --> 00:27:34.115
consuming, and makes a huge difference.

00:27:34.908 --> 00:27:36.168
Now monitoring.

00:27:36.818 --> 00:27:44.541
Monitoring is the process of having
periodic times that your website

00:27:44.649 --> 00:27:48.042
is, is monitored for any changes.

00:27:48.621 --> 00:27:54.052
You know, I could remediate a whole
site today, and if somebody in your

00:27:54.056 --> 00:28:00.153
organization decides to put up a new
page that's not compliant, without

00:28:00.226 --> 00:28:06.138
monitoring, we can't find that pa-
that, that, that item that needs

00:28:06.139 --> 00:28:13.344
to be remediated until we go and
monitor it again for identification.

00:28:14.111 --> 00:28:21.187
And because many of you have multiple
people adding things to the website under

00:28:21.187 --> 00:28:27.942
the monitoring phase, we also recommend
that you create one individual that

00:28:28.369 --> 00:28:34.918
checks everything or signs off on things,
or at least have a policy framework

00:28:35.379 --> 00:28:40.454
for those that are uploading as to what
they can upload and what they have to--

00:28:40.489 --> 00:28:43.122
what checklist they have to go through.

00:28:43.592 --> 00:28:50.566
So monitoring is not just monitoring the
site, but it's monitoring how things get

00:28:50.606 --> 00:28:53.609
on the site and providing that checklist.

00:28:54.215 --> 00:28:56.187
And then improve.

00:28:56.964 --> 00:29:04.688
Um, your site from these three, first
evaluation, remediation, and monitoring,

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:10.879
will improve over time, and then look
for more and more ways to improve.

00:29:11.464 --> 00:29:18.902
You know, the better experience you give
everyone on your site, the more revenue,

00:29:19.001 --> 00:29:25.566
engagement, et cetera, will be delivered,
and you'll be the ultimate beneficiary

00:29:25.581 --> 00:29:27.774
because you made it easy for everyone.

00:29:28.275 --> 00:29:31.033
So again, it, it's not a one-time project.

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:37.944
The website changes, the content
changes, plugins change, the

00:29:37.945 --> 00:29:39.991
teams that do the work change.

00:29:40.566 --> 00:29:46.302
The organizations that succeed
treat accessibility as continuous

00:29:46.303 --> 00:29:50.838
improvement, not a panic
project after something breaks.

00:29:56.859 --> 00:30:01.597
So let's talk about
accessibility as a conversation.

00:30:02.686 --> 00:30:04.691
It is a conversation.

00:30:05.173 --> 00:30:11.943
It builds trust, and trust creates
engagement, engagement creates

00:30:11.967 --> 00:30:15.817
loyalty, and loyalty creates advocacy.

00:30:17.141 --> 00:30:23.725
For associations, that can translate
into stronger member relationships

00:30:23.779 --> 00:30:26.389
and longer membership life cycles.

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:32.520
Think if you have those older members
that are in the legacy portion of their

00:30:32.531 --> 00:30:38.928
career, and you make it simpler for them
to use your site, which now increases

00:30:38.929 --> 00:30:41.029
their lifetime membership value.

00:30:41.470 --> 00:30:47.463
But then at some point, because they're
in the legacy portion of their career,

00:30:47.478 --> 00:30:54.112
you could convert them to a large legacy
gift, uh, or even a small legacy gift.

00:30:54.756 --> 00:30:59.716
So as they phase out of their
professional career, you can

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:05.977
still enhance your revenue for the
association to create added benefits.

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:12.003
Uh, for nonprofits, it helps
strengthen these donor relationships.

00:31:12.484 --> 00:31:17.346
Um, and for corporations, you're
gonna have customers that not

00:31:17.347 --> 00:31:20.365
only return, but also recommend.

00:31:20.858 --> 00:31:23.911
You wouldn't believe how many
times I tell people about the

00:31:23.912 --> 00:31:29.822
great experiences that I've had
and why people should be utilizing

00:31:30.039 --> 00:31:32.640
and patronizing these businesses.

00:31:33.242 --> 00:31:40.454
This is why accessibility belongs
in strategic conversations, not

00:31:40.456 --> 00:31:43.188
just technical or legal ones.

00:31:43.811 --> 00:31:49.984
It's how organizations become
known, liked, and trusted.

00:31:51.517 --> 00:31:52.540
Continue, please.

00:31:55.925 --> 00:31:58.272
So what should organizations do next?

00:31:58.668 --> 00:32:00.418
Mark, start small.

00:32:01.511 --> 00:32:04.157
You can't eat an elephant all in one bite.

00:32:05.238 --> 00:32:11.905
Start small and measure the
progress, continuously improving.

00:32:13.151 --> 00:32:18.545
Like I say, you can't fix everything all
at once or overnight, but you do need to

00:32:18.552 --> 00:32:22.142
begin, and those beginnings can be simple.

00:32:22.973 --> 00:32:28.080
What if we worked on our alt text
or our buttons and what they say?

00:32:28.449 --> 00:32:33.712
What if we worked on
our headings, H1s, H2s?

00:32:34.141 --> 00:32:38.226
What if we made sure that
we had the right contrast?

00:32:39.001 --> 00:32:45.378
Those are little things that we can do
to get started, and they immediately

00:32:45.385 --> 00:32:51.457
impact on how your site performs,
and then gives you the opportunity

00:32:51.822 --> 00:32:57.101
to review your site and look for
additional areas of improvement.

00:32:57.929 --> 00:33:02.342
Again, you don't need to fix everything
overnight, but you do need to begin,

00:33:02.575 --> 00:33:08.346
and you need a process that keeps
accessibility from being ignored

00:33:08.435 --> 00:33:10.795
after the first round of fixes.

00:33:13.601 --> 00:33:19.787
Accessibility, it's not about
limitations, it's about possibilities.

00:33:20.783 --> 00:33:31.791
It's about making sure more people can
participate, contribute, buy, join, learn,

00:33:32.121 --> 00:33:36.481
register, renew, donate, and engage.

00:33:36.941 --> 00:33:44.975
When we design accessibility and we design
for access, we expand the opportunities

00:33:47.081 --> 00:33:53.242
Now, if you found value in what
we're talking about today in the

00:33:53.242 --> 00:33:59.978
presentation and would like to continue
the conversation, this is why I

00:33:59.978 --> 00:34:02.030
asked you to get your phone handy.

