where you, you know, you found out

00:41:21.651 --> 00:41:24.714
about a, a company having a problem,
and you told them about the benefits

00:41:24.714 --> 00:41:28.615
that you mentioned in your presentation,
and it, and you turned it into a, a

00:41:28.616 --> 00:41:31.866
win-win for them, and y- you got some
great numbers that you could share?

00:41:31.997 --> 00:41:35.064
Is there any, uh, sort of success story
you could share with us like that?

00:41:35.611 --> 00:41:39.966
John: I, I can give you a, a couple
success stories, uh, on that, but,

00:41:40.326 --> 00:41:45.470
but one is, um, some work that we're
doing right now, uh, and potentially

00:41:45.473 --> 00:41:47.332
doing for another organization.

00:41:47.805 --> 00:41:52.560
So much information is buried in PDFs.

00:41:53.375 --> 00:41:58.397
PDFs are not structured
to be read by agents.

00:41:58.788 --> 00:42:02.165
They're not well used on telephones.

00:42:02.566 --> 00:42:09.281
Um, so converting PDFs to HTML
now does many, many things.

00:42:09.281 --> 00:42:12.299
First of all, it allows you to
have a responsive document so

00:42:12.299 --> 00:42:17.522
people on their phone can utilize
it, um, and, and actually read it.

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:24.270
But secondly, because the agents can
now read it, you start to see those PDFs

00:42:24.604 --> 00:42:32.183
becoming sources and credible sources
for search engines, AEO, and GEO.

00:42:32.536 --> 00:42:35.011
And I don't have actual
numbers in front of me.

00:42:35.011 --> 00:42:40.946
Uh, unfortunately, it's almost 10 o'clock
on the East Coast where I'm at, so- … so

00:42:40.946 --> 00:42:43.489
I, I, I didn't have everything available.

00:42:43.489 --> 00:42:49.413
But the reality is we've seen
dramatic increases in organizations.

00:42:49.915 --> 00:42:57.365
Discoverability, um, which we know
converts directly to more engagement,

00:42:57.377 --> 00:43:03.671
more revenue, and, uh, paying for
that expense that they originally

00:43:03.682 --> 00:43:07.277
thought was an expense, which really
turns out to be an investment.

00:43:08.088 --> 00:43:08.537
Joe: Perfect.

00:43:08.602 --> 00:43:09.348
No, I love that.

00:43:09.529 --> 00:43:13.544
And I loved how it, um, how you offered
to be there to listen and, and to,

00:43:13.899 --> 00:43:15.149
uh, share your wisdom with people.

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:18.272
This is, this is what WordPress
Accessibility Day, why it's

00:43:18.272 --> 00:43:19.396
so awesome in my opinion.

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:23.921
All right, our next question: What's the
difference between how video captions

00:43:23.921 --> 00:43:26.216
and tran- and transcripts are used?

00:43:26.703 --> 00:43:27.992
Are they both needed?

00:43:29.982 --> 00:43:31.921
John: Uh, great question.

00:43:32.909 --> 00:43:41.439
I would say both are excellent because
people are different, and each one of

00:43:41.441 --> 00:43:46.721
them wants to receive information in
the way that's comfortable for them.

00:43:47.397 --> 00:43:48.642
I'll give you an example.

00:43:48.955 --> 00:43:54.710
Um, ALS should be really named
constant state of adaptation, because

00:43:55.297 --> 00:43:59.662
my body keeps changing, and I'm
on a constant l- learning curve.

00:44:00.014 --> 00:44:05.290
So a lot of these, quote unquote,
"accessible tools" out there, I don't

00:44:05.290 --> 00:44:10.095
have the energy to learn them, but I
have no problem watching closed captions.

00:44:10.607 --> 00:44:13.145
I do have difficulty reading transcripts.

00:44:14.035 --> 00:44:16.788
But others are just the opposite.

00:44:17.509 --> 00:44:21.717
So, so both are great.

00:44:22.379 --> 00:44:25.045
Without either, it's a mistake.

00:44:25.615 --> 00:44:31.317
And in today's technology age, both
are not that difficult to perform.

00:44:33.301 --> 00:44:35.786
Joe: And it's a matter of preference,
so that's, that's a perfect answer.

00:44:36.083 --> 00:44:39.310
Our next question is, are there
governance plug-ins or accessibility

00:44:39.321 --> 00:44:44.063
plug-ins you can recommend to help
maintain content and keep it accessible?

00:44:45.524 --> 00:44:49.758
John: Um, there are plug-ins, and
unfortunately I don't have the ex- exact

00:44:49.769 --> 00:44:52.578
plug-ins, uh, off the top of my head.

00:44:52.910 --> 00:44:52.935
Mm-hmm.

00:44:52.935 --> 00:44:56.971
However, my, my dev team would
be happy to, to get me a list.

00:44:56.971 --> 00:45:01.477
So if you put your, uh, a-
contact information, we'll

00:45:01.477 --> 00:45:02.488
be happy to get you that.

00:45:02.499 --> 00:45:06.866
That said, though, uh, it
really boils down to a process,

00:45:07.737 --> 00:45:09.857
setting you up for a process.

00:45:10.268 --> 00:45:17.036
For example, everybody can create content
on your site, but it should all go

00:45:17.036 --> 00:45:22.761
through one checkpoint to make sure that
before it goes on your site, that it's…

00:45:23.357 --> 00:45:28.366
that it meets the guidelines that the
organization is trying to, to create.

00:45:28.806 --> 00:45:33.476
So it doesn't mean one person has
to be the ultimate content person,

00:45:33.756 --> 00:45:39.430
but one person has to own the, own
the digital assets that go on there.

00:45:39.731 --> 00:45:45.270
Um, and I'll be happy to get the plug-ins
that we rely on in- internally to you,

00:45:45.308 --> 00:45:47.839
uh, via email if you put your email there.

00:45:48.926 --> 00:45:49.337
Joe: Perfect.

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:49.797
Perfect.

00:45:50.109 --> 00:45:53.185
Um, a couple more questions with the
t- I mean, I wish I could just keep

00:45:53.417 --> 00:45:56.298
throwing these questions at you,
um, but we're almost out of time.

00:45:56.449 --> 00:46:00.118
Can you describe how you use a
computer with voice control, and

00:46:00.141 --> 00:46:04.211
are there specific things developers
can do to make websites work better

00:46:04.497 --> 00:46:06.116
for people who use voice control?

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:11.689
John: Well, first I've got… If
Apple is listening, 'cause I'm an

00:46:11.694 --> 00:46:16.236
Apple guy because it has… Uh, but
if Apple's listening, one thing that

00:46:16.236 --> 00:46:24.332
they could do is when y- They put a
number up that represents something.

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:30.199
For example, for those of you who don't
know voice control, um, everything that

00:46:30.199 --> 00:46:35.115
could be acted upon when I have voice
control on has a number show up for it.

00:46:35.928 --> 00:46:40.813
But if something changes on the page,
they reorder all the numbers instead

00:46:40.813 --> 00:46:46.228
of keeping the numbers constant and
adding new numbers for the new things.

00:46:46.603 --> 00:46:50.295
So that's one thing you gotta be
careful about when using voice control.

00:46:50.679 --> 00:46:55.835
I'm too fast for it sometimes, so
I end up going down a, a, a path.

00:46:55.851 --> 00:46:59.047
So using voice control, be patient.

00:46:59.815 --> 00:47:06.875
Uh, to let you know this, Zoom, uh, Google
Meet, a lot of the microphones tend to

00:47:07.123 --> 00:47:13.550
take hostage over your microphone, so you
have difficulty using voice control with

00:47:13.668 --> 00:47:16.265
some of the other, uh, tools out there.

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:22.669
Uh, I've gotten around that by using
a combination of my phone for sh-

00:47:22.742 --> 00:47:27.904
uh, reading information while my
computer was used for the meeting.

00:47:28.664 --> 00:47:33.666
Um, I learned some great things
recently that I can talk to each

00:47:33.669 --> 00:47:37.893
device as to letting them know
who's being active and who isn't.

00:47:38.241 --> 00:47:42.447
So I could have multiple… I could
have my phone and, uh, the computer

00:47:43.005 --> 00:47:47.770
both working in tandem by telling who
to wake up and who to go to sleep.

00:47:47.771 --> 00:47:51.202
So, uh, play with it.

00:47:51.446 --> 00:47:54.839
Every one of you that has an Apple
product can play with it today.

00:47:55.176 --> 00:47:59.595
Play with it, and if you're able-bodied,
I could see you using it as well.

00:48:00.600 --> 00:48:01.054
Joe: Perfect.

00:48:01.316 --> 00:48:01.713
Perfect.

00:48:01.949 --> 00:48:04.394
Uh, now this isn't a
presentation related question.

00:48:04.394 --> 00:48:09.460
It's, um, as someone living with
ALS, what website a- accessibility

00:48:09.462 --> 00:48:11.391
features matter most to you?

00:48:13.248 --> 00:48:19.156
John: Um, don't put a slider
on your, on your, uh, website.

00:48:19.717 --> 00:48:24.427
Or, or if you do, give me an,
a chance to not have to use it.

00:48:25.252 --> 00:48:29.336
Uh, I'll give you a,
a, a personal example.

00:48:29.590 --> 00:48:37.187
Um, until I learned I could tell voice
control how many grid, how many columns

00:48:37.187 --> 00:48:44.892
and rows I wanted, um, I couldn't get
a fine enough grid pattern to, uh, do

00:48:44.911 --> 00:48:49.643
the activities on my SoFi app because
it required me to drag something from

00:48:49.643 --> 00:48:54.379
left to right, and I could never grab
it where it was positioned on the phone.

00:48:54.881 --> 00:48:56.724
So that's the one thing.

00:48:56.990 --> 00:49:04.265
The other thing is, um, when you're
designing things, look at it with

00:49:04.303 --> 00:49:07.778
voice control and see where they land.

00:49:08.279 --> 00:49:14.950
Because if they land uniquely
outside of the grid, now we're having

00:49:14.953 --> 00:49:19.198
to go press eight, press seven,
press … We're having to go three

00:49:19.223 --> 00:49:21.483
commands to get to that one area.

00:49:21.808 --> 00:49:26.157
Um, so design it so they're falling
right on where the commands are.

00:49:26.525 --> 00:49:28.757
Um, but those are just a couple things.

00:49:29.381 --> 00:49:30.950
Play with the voice control.

00:49:31.187 --> 00:49:34.487
As a developer, if you're a
developer, play with it if,

00:49:34.580 --> 00:49:36.278
if you've got an Apple device.

00:49:36.309 --> 00:49:40.640
Your, your Mac or your phone
both will allow you to do that.

00:49:40.682 --> 00:49:44.586
You don't need a special download,
and you don't need a special tool.

00:49:46.255 --> 00:49:49.916
Joe: It wa- it was s- such an honor and
pleasure to be with you for the last hour,

00:49:49.921 --> 00:49:51.974
Mark, and, and hear your presentation.

00:49:51.975 --> 00:49:54.354
Thank you so much for
being here with us today.

00:49:54.715 --> 00:49:55.430
John: Thank you, Joe.

00:49:55.432 --> 00:49:57.862
You were awesome, and I
really appreciate everyone.

